\itemize{
\item \code{theta_grid}: matrix or vector of grid hyperparameter values.
\item \code{log_marginal}: log p(y, mode | theta_k) at each grid point.
\item \code{weights}: integration weights normalising to sum 1.
\item \code{theta_mean}, \code{theta_sd}: posterior moments per hyperparameter.
\item \code{n_iter}: inner Newton iterations per grid point.
\item \code{modes}: matrix \verb{[n_grid x n_x]} of inner modes, when stored.
\item \code{pareto_k}, \code{pareto_k_is_ess}: outer Pareto-\eqn{\hat{k}} and its
importance-sampling ESS (\code{NA} when not computed for the grid; see
\code{control$diagnose_k}).
\item \code{pareto_k_proposal_source}, \code{pareto_k_first_pass}: which proposal family
the reported k-hat came from (the outer k-hat is scored against several
candidates and the best is kept), and the k-hat of the FIRST pass -- the
proposal exactly as this backend placed it, before any candidate refined
or replaced it. A large gap between the two says the nodes are badly
scaled around the hyperparameter posterior even though the verdict is
fine; no gap says the placement was already right.
\item \code{inner_skew}, \code{inner_skew_idx}, \code{inner_skew_dropped}: the inner-Laplace
skewness diagnostic (gamma_3) at each scored latent index and its
1-based index, plus a count of (index, observation) contributions
dropped for a non-finite third derivative (see \code{control$diagnose_skew}).
\item \code{timing}: named numeric of wall-clock seconds (\code{total}, \code{setup},
\code{grid}, \code{postproc}, \code{diagnostics}); the \code{grid} phase is the inner
Laplace pass that scales with grid size. Surfaced one-line in \code{print}.
\item \code{prune_cheap_log_marginal}, \code{prune_mask}, \code{prune_n_pruned}, \code{prune_tol}:
present only when \code{control$prune = TRUE} and the safety gate did not
trip -- the cheap-screen log-marginal per cell, the logical mask of
pruned cells, the pruned-cell count, and the threshold actually applied.
\item \code{prune_fallback_triggered}, \code{prune_fallback_reason}: present only when
the gate did trip; the rest of the fit is then the full-grid result and
the reason string records which check fired.
\item \code{prior}: echoed input.
}
}
\description{
Generic outer-grid nested Laplace driver. Builds a grid over the
hyperparameters of a single latent prior block (spatial or temporal),
runs an inner Laplace at each grid point with warm-starting, and
integrates over the grid to give proper hyperparameter marginals.

Supported priors:
\itemize{
\item Spatial (areal): \code{"icar"} (1D grid on tau), \code{"bym2"}
(2D on (sigma, rho)), \code{"car_proper"} (2D on (tau, rho); rho lives
in the eigenvalue interval (1/lambda_min, 1/lambda_max) of
\verb{D^\{-1\} W}).
\item Spatial (continuous): \code{"nngp"} (2D on (sigma2, phi_gp)),
\code{"hsgp"} (2D on (sigma2, lengthscale)).
\item Temporal: \code{"rw1"}, \code{"rw2"} (1D grid on tau), \code{"ar1"} (2D on (tau, rho))
\item SPDE continuous spatial: see \code{cpp_nested_laplace_spde()} (separate
entry, rebuilds Q via SPDE Q-builder).
}
}
\examples{
\donttest{
set.seed(1)
S <- 30L                                   # spatial units arranged in a chain
nb <- lapply(seq_len(S), function(s) setdiff(c(s - 1L, s + 1L), c(0L, S + 1L)))
nn <- lengths(nb)
field <- as.numeric(scale(cumsum(rnorm(S, 0, 0.4))))   # smooth spatial field
idx <- rep(seq_len(S), each = 6L); n <- length(idx); x <- rnorm(n)
y <- rbinom(n, 1L, plogis(-0.2 + 0.6 * x + field[idx]))
prior <- list(type = "icar", n_spatial_units = S, spatial_idx = idx,
              adj_row_ptr = c(0L, cumsum(nn)), adj_col_idx = unlist(nb) - 1L,
              n_neighbors = nn, tau_grid = c(0.5, 1, 2, 4, 8))
fit <- tulpa_nested_laplace(y, rep(1L, n), cbind(1, x), prior = prior,
                            family = "binomial")
fit$theta_mean        # marginalized ICAR precision
}
}
\references{
Rue, Martino & Chopin (2009). Approximate Bayesian inference for latent
Gaussian models by using integrated nested Laplace approximations.
\emph{JRSS-B} 71(2):319-392.
}
